/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 101 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `"From benefit." The Shach wrote that it is explained in the Gemara that prohibitions involving benefit — such as basar b'chalav and the like — if they are a piece worthy of honor, prohibit their mixture also from benefit, even in [a ratio of] one thousand. However, it is permitted to sell them to a non-Jew, except for the value of the prohibition contained within them.`,
    '1#ב': `"Leniently." For this stringency regarding a piece worthy of honor is only d'rabbanan. And see what was written at the beginning of siman 100.`,
    '2#א': `"With milk." Because both of them have become one body — and the proof is that one receives lashes from the Torah if one ate approximately half an olive-measure of milk and half an olive-measure of meat. End of the words of the Taz.`,
    '2#ב': `"Taste." For that which is absorbed is not worthy of honor. Based on this, the Torat Chatat wrote that a cheese curdled with treifah milk that subsequently became mixed in [with other cheese] is not a piece worthy of honor, since the prohibition within it is absorbed. The Bach was puzzled by this, for the Torat Chatat had ruled regarding non-Jewish cheese that a double doubt (sfek sfeika) does not help, since the prohibition is inherent to the cheese itself — and if so, here too he should have ruled that it is a piece worthy of honor. The Taz answered that there the discussion concerns cheese in which there is concern that non-kosher milk became mixed in and there was no nullification by a majority of kosher milk, and it teaches us that regarding a double doubt they were stringent, since the Sages prohibited it from the outset due to a d'oraisa doubt, and it is therefore called a certain treifah — for the first doubt is counted as certain. Whereas here the discussion is where there is a majority sufficient to nullify the prohibition, but you come to prohibit it due to the rule of a piece worthy of honor — regarding this I say that it is only an absorbed prohibition, and we do not apply the rule of a piece worthy of honor to it. He further wrote: nevertheless, it appears to me that we consider ordinary non-Jewish cheese as an absorbed prohibition, and it is nullified, and we do not say that it comes from treifah animal milk, since we follow the majority — see there. However, what he wrote at the end of his words — that if there is concern that the cheese was curdled with the stomach-lining of a neveilah, due to this it has the status of a piece worthy of honor, for there is a basar b'chalav prohibition here, end of his words — does not seem correct to me. For the reason we apply the rule of a piece worthy of honor to basar b'chalav is because each one of them is permitted on its own, and this logic does not apply to neveilah. Furthermore, it contradicts what the Mechaber wrote in siman 87, seif 11 — see there, and consider this carefully. It is possible, however, to press his words to mean that the prohibition of neveilah is a prohibition inherent to itself, and we apply a piece worthy of honor to it just as with basar b'chalav, which is also called a prohibition inherent to itself. And it is straightforward.`,
    '2#ג': `"Peeling." The Shach wrote that this rule is only found regarding basar b'chalav, but regarding other prohibitions they were only prohibited due to absorbed taste, and it is obvious that they are not called a piece worthy of honor. However, there are decisors who hold that if one were to peel it, it would be a piece worthy of honor that is not nullified — see there.`,
    '3#א': `"Worthy of honor." The Shach wrote that it is also not a counted item (davar shebeminyan), since it lacks such a significant degree of preparation. This is also the opinion of the Bach, unlike the Torat Chatat who wrote that it is a counted item. And see below, siman 110.`,
    '3#ב': `"They removed it." Nevertheless, since at the time of the initial mixture it was nullified, it does not revert to being prohibited when stirred. Sefer Mitzvot Katan.`,
    '3#ג': `"Whole." Even if its skin was removed.`,
    '3#ד': `"In its feathers." The same law applies to a sheep in its skin, and the like.`,
    '3#ה': `"Goose." The Torat Chatat wrote that goose legs whose skin has not been singed off them, as is the custom to do, are comparable to a chicken in its feathers. The Taz wrote that it implies that if the feathers were already removed but the innards had not yet been taken out and the belly had not been opened, it is still considered a piece worthy of honor and is not nullified. Maharshal wrote that the head of an animal is not considered worthy of honor, except for the head of a calf — and certainly the tongue of an ox is considered worthy of honor, for Abraham honored the angels with three tongues.`,
    '4#א': `"The gizzard." The Taz wrote that for us, the outer membrane of the calf's gizzard is considered a piece worthy of honor.`,
    '4#ב': `"Fat." The Shach wrote that Maharshal disagrees with this [and wrote that the outer membrane of the calf's gizzard is considered (a piece worthy of honor)]. Since these laws vary according to place and time, as will be seen below at the beginning of siman 110, I have been brief in this matter, and everything depends on the judgment of the decisor whether it is considered significant in that place or not; and if there is doubt, we are lenient. The Taz wrote that one may not be lenient regarding the skin of goose fat, even if it was cut into strips (called gribn), for even so it is called a piece worthy of honor. (And in Nekudat HaKesef he disagrees with this, for one does not honor a guest with one piece alone but only with many together, and if so, each individual piece by itself is not worthy of honor — see there.)`,
    '5#_': `"Other." The Taz wrote in the name of Maharshal that the gizzard of a goose is considered a piece worthy of honor, and it implies that the gizzard of a chicken is not a piece worthy of honor. Maharshal further wrote that the sages of Krakow ruled that the goose breast meat called bilak — even though the skin was removed from it — is still worthy of honor, and he disagrees with this. The Taz's conclusion is that it is called a piece worthy of honor on the grounds that any piece currently worthy of honor, even though it was formerly even more significant, does not have its significance nullified. The Levush wrote that those goose livers that turned white due to the fat are certainly significant and are not nullified. The main point in this matter is that it depends on the place and time.`,
    '6#א': `"Were crushed." The Torat Chatat wrote that it is implied in the Issur ve'Heter that the ruling that a beriah is nullified if it was crushed means that no significant item remained there; but if a piece worthy of honor remained, even though it was nullified due to the rule of beriah, nevertheless it is still considered significant and is not nullified due to the rule of a piece worthy of honor — and this is a correct reasoning, end of his words. (As is also written [there]: and correspondingly, what commonly falls off slightly through cooking is not considered crushing; but if it separated slightly from its body and from its spine or its head, that is considered crushing. And crushing is effective for any significant item, except for something that has a permitted aspect (davar she'yesh lo matirin) — see there.)`,
    '6#ב': `"Of their own kind." The Taz explained: for example, a pot of vegetables in which a beriah was lost — everything is prohibited. And see below, siman 102, seif 1.`,
    '7#א': `"Is permitted." The Issur ve'Heter wrote that this ruling speaks of when there are sixty [parts] of permitted food against the prohibited part; and what the Mechaber wrote, 'if it is of the prohibited type,' means that even the prohibited item is included within this doubt — namely, that perhaps it was cut.`,
    '7#ב': `"We attribute it." Even though everywhere we follow the majority, nevertheless here we say that anything that has changed from its original state is attributed to the majority, and it is like the rule of 'anything that separates is attributed to the majority,' etc. Annotation of Shaarei Dura.`,
    '9#_': `"Similar." The Taz wrote that it implies that they must also be equal in appearance. And even though regarding the fat of the gizzard the appearance of the fat alone suffices, here it is different — since it is possible to verify that they are also aligned in the cut, this is also required. The Issur ve'Heter wrote that nowadays, when we are not so expert in this, we cannot permit in such a case unless there is a large and obvious difference. The Bach wrote that he received this teaching from a certain sage: if fluid was found in the head of one sheep that would render it treifah, and it was not known from which sheep the head came, one should examine the spinal cord of the sheep — and whichever sheep has fluid in its head will have fluid in the cord extending from the brain to the spine, and we attribute the head to that sheep in order to permit the others. End of his words. The Taz concluded that in this matter it is not worthwhile to permit a doubtful treifah status on this basis, since it is not found in the Gemara or the decisors. And see above, siman 83.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `The conclusion of the Gemara on the Mishnah regarding a piece of neveilah, etc. — Chullin daf 100.`,
    '1#ב': `Tosafot, the Rosh, and the Ran there, from the Mishnah of those that are forbidden, etc., and basar b'chalav, etc. — Avodah Zarah daf 74.`,
    '2#_': `The Rosh there; and so wrote the Rashba, the Ran, and other poskim. The reason is that the prohibition within it is not worthy of honor. And as for basar b'chalav — if it is worthy of honor it is not nullified — that case is different, because since they were cooked together with one another, the whole reverts to being as one body, as written above in siman 92, seif 4.`,
    '3#א': `Tur in the name of his father the Rosh, in the name of some who say, there at the end of the chapter of Gid HaNashe; and the Rashba, the Ran, and other poskim; and so wrote the Semak and Sha'ar Dura in the name of an incident that came before Rabbeinu Tam.`,
    '3#ב': `There, in his rulings.`,
    '3#ג': `There; and so wrote the Rashba in Torat HaBayit, and the Ran, of blessed memory.`,
    '3#ד': `Tur in the name of his father, the Rosh.`,
    '4#_': `Beit Yosef in the name of a found written source; and Issur VeHeter.`,
    '5#_': `Tur in siman 114, and Sefer Mitzvot Gadol, Negative Commandment 141, in the name of Rav Yehuda.`,
    '6#א': `The Rosh at the end of the chapter of Gid HaNashe, from the case of one who crushed nine ants while one was alive, etc. — Makkot daf 16; and in the name of a responsum of the Rif; and in the name of Tosafot; and so wrote the Rashba and the Ran there.`,
    '6#ב': `The Rosh and the Ran, from that which I cited in siman 99, seif 5.`,
    '7#_': `The Rosh and the Ran there.`,
    '8#_': `Hagahot Maimoniyot in chapter 7 of Hilchot Shechita, in the name of an incident that came before Rabbi, etc., from that which I cited above in siman 83, seif 4; (and Hagahot Asheri at the beginning of the chapter of Eilu Tereifot, in the name of Or Zarua).`,
    '9#_': `Terumat HaDeshen, siman 179.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Its law is like [that of] a beriah. As stated [above]: 'and likewise etc.' and 'it is necessary etc.' — as discussed above (in siman 100, se'if katan 5).`,
    '1#ב': `That even etc. — As noted above from the Tosefta and the Gemara at the beginning of chapter 8 of Zevachim, and chapter 1 of [tractate] Yom Tov (Beitzah), and chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah (daf 74a): 'and these are forbidden etc.' And [the Gemara] challenges there: 'let it also teach [the case of] a piece of neveilah etc.' — see Tosafot there, s.v. 'l'me'utei etc.' And for this reason it appears [the word] comes to exclude etc. And likewise the Bach there [holds this is so] because it is a piece worthy of honor (chaticha ha'ra'uya l'hitchabed), which is a thing [prohibited by rabbinic] count (davar shebeminyan). See Tosafot there, s.v. 'ha etc.,' and in Chullin 100a, s.v. 'sha'ani etc.,' and one can explain that this is its meaning etc.`,
    '1#ג': `And even if it is etc. — See the aforementioned Tosafot in Avodah Zarah, s.v. 'ha,' and in Chullin there, s.v. 'sha'ani etc.'`,
    '1#ד': `Since if etc. and it is worthy of honor. That is to say, to exclude pieces of a sin-offering [discussed] in the chapter ha'Areil — for even if they were to become nullified, they remain forbidden to non-priests, and to priests they are not worthy of honor. See the aforementioned Tosafot in Chullin, s.v. [above], and in Yevamot, chapter 1, daf 20, s.v. 'divrei etc.'`,
    '1#ה': `And even if it is not etc. — As is written regarding yayin nesech in Avodah Zarah. See the aforementioned Tosafot, s.v. 'ha etc.,' and [their question]: 'and if so, yayin nesech etc.' And in Yevamot, chapter 1, daf 1, s.v. 'mai etc.'`,
    '1#ו': `And if it is doubtful etc. — As will be discussed in siman 110, seif 1, in the Rama's gloss, [the rule] that something of importance etc.; and as written in Gittin 54b — see Rashi there, s.v. 'lo ya'alu,' and Tosafot in Zevachim 73b, s.v. 'ela etc.'`,
    '2#א': `It has no [nullification] etc. — From what is taught in the Mishnah in Chullin 96b: 'and likewise a piece etc., and likewise a piece etc.' — those specific [cases] alone are enumerated precisely. [Collection:] It has no [nullification] etc. — For it is not superior to a species mixed with its own species according to Rav Yehuda, where it is not nullified d'oraisa, and yet even so in such a case it is nullified — except in basar b'chalav. See Tosafot, Chullin 108b, s.v. 'amai etc.' (end of quote).`,
    '2#ב': `Such as neveilah — in the chapter Gid HaNasheh and there.`,
    '2#ג': `And in basar b'chalav — there.`,
    '3#א': `A chicken etc., and likewise etc., and likewise etc. — From the fact that [the Gemara] in Zevachim there challenges: 'let them be nullified by a majority!' — even though there are many pieces worthy of honor (chatichot ha're'uyot l'hitchabed) among them — since as it is [in its whole, intact state] it is not [considered] worthy of honor. And likewise in the Yerushalmi, which Tosafot cite in Yevamot there, [the Gemara] interprets the Mishnah of Avodah Zarah as referring to a slaughtered ox [and the Rash in chapter 3 of Orlah wrote in the name of the Yerushalmi: in a cut piece]. And even though in our Gemara in Zevachim there it appears to refer to a living animal, nevertheless it states there that it is specifically because it is a living creature (ba'al chayim) that it is not nullified.`,
    '3#ב': `And even though etc. — As noted in siman 99, seif 6, that in such a case [the forbidden status] does not revive [once nullified]. See there.`,
    '3#ג': `And some disagree with all of this — see Tosafot in Zevachim 72a, s.v. 'ela etc.,' and in Yevamot chapter 1, daf 2, [where] Rabbi Yochanan etc. And the proof is from the fact that [the Talmud] counts among the six things: beets, cabbage, and gourd — and these are not normally cooked whole, hence it must be referring to them when alive. And likewise in Avodah Zarah, which counts the birds of the metzora etc. — [this is the view of] the Rosh. And according to this, even a chicken in its feathers is like the birds of the metzora.`,
    '3#ד': `And so the practice is, except for etc., and likewise etc. — [This follows] the first position, except that one can say that the ox condemned to stoning is different, since it lacks a major rectification [before it could ever become permitted].`,
    '3#ה': `And likewise fish etc. — See Bava Batra, chapter 3, [daf] 2, in the Mishnah.`,
    '5#_': `The gizzard and likewise etc. — As written in [tractate] Yom Tov (Beitzah) 7a, and Nedarim 54b, and Meilah 20b.`,
    '6#א': `This etc. — Beriah was explained in the preceding siman; and piece worthy of honor (chaticha ha'ra'uya l'hitchabed) is as stated in the aforementioned Tosefta: 'a forbidden piece that became mixed among [other] pieces — even if they number a thousand, they are all forbidden' — and the gravy [is assessed] by noten taam (imparting flavor). If it dissolved, it [is assessed] by noten taam. And in Yevamot chapter 1, daf 2, [the case of] when it dissolved. See Tosafot there, s.v. 'divrei etc.,' and in the Mishnah, chapter 2 of Orlah: 'how [is it if] they were crushed etc.'`,
    '6#ב': `Until it is lost etc. — This applies only to piece worthy of honor (chaticha ha'ra'uya l'hitchabed), as stated in the preceding siman.`,
    '6#ג': `And even if etc. — Gittin 54b: 'they fell [and were] crushed etc.' See Rashi there, s.v. 'naflu etc.'`,
    '6#ד': `And even after etc. — From the fact that it states there [the rule concerning one who acted] deliberately etc. And as noted in siman 99, seif 5, in the Rama's gloss. See there.`,
    '6#ה': `And that is when they were crushed etc. — Gittin there.`,
    '6#ו': `To crush etc., and likewise etc. — As noted in siman 99, seif 5. See there.`,
    '6#ז': `And there is no distinction etc. — The location of this Rama's gloss is at the beginning of this siman. That is to say, a piece worthy of honor (chaticha ha'ra'uya l'hitchabed) is not nullified even among things not of its own species (she'eino mino), contrary to the Or Zarua, who compares beriah and piece worthy of honor to something that has a permitted aspect (davar she'yesh lo matirin). See above, siman 100, seif 3 and seif 4. And as written in the chapter 'Elu Trefot' (daf 67a): 'a person should not skin etc.' And even though presumably there is more than sixty times [the permitted material] relative to it — (according to Rashi there this is not a proof — see the end of siman 100). And see what is written at the beginning of siman 110. See there.`,
    '7#_': `If it was cut etc., and even etc. — Zevachim 74b.`,
    '8#_': `The gizzard etc., the head etc. — Avodah Zarah 40a.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `1) [Seif 1] "A piece worthy of honor, etc." — meaning it is fit to be served with honor before guests. Chullin 100a. Tur. Levush. Kneset HaGedolah in Hagahot HaTur, note 1. And we require that it be fit to be served with honor before guests of the sort that distinguished people are accustomed to serve it. Maharshal, Perek Gid HaNasheh, siman 23. Kneset HaGedolah ibid. Beit Lechem Yehudah note 1. Mishbetzot Zahav note 1, who wrote that this means honored and important guests. Beit Yitzchak in Amudei Zahav, note 3. Aruch HaShulchan note 1.`,
    '2#_': `2) And if you ask: how is it considered a piece worthy of honor — is it not neveilah or tereifah? — this is not a question at all. For not only is neveilah and tereifah fit to be served before a non-Jewish guest, but even a piece of something prohibited in benefit, such as basar b'chalav, is also considered worthy of honor — since, if we were to say that it is nullified and permitted, it would then be worthy of honor. Therefore it is not nullified for this reason. Aruch HaShulchan ibid.`,
    '3#_': `3) And specifically the piece itself is not nullified, but its taste is nevertheless nullified in sixty, as was written above (in the previous siman) regarding the law of beriah. Levush.`,
    '4#_': `4) Ibid. "And even if it is prohibited in benefit, etc." — Prohibited-in-benefit items such as basar b'chalav and similar cases, if they are pieces worthy of honor, render their mixture forbidden in benefit as well, even in a thousand [parts]. However, it is permitted to sell them to a non-Jew, except for the monetary value of the forbidden portion within them. Shach, small note 1. Peri Chadash note 1. Minhat Yaakov on the Torat Chatat, klal 40, note 1. Beit Lechem Yehudah note 2. Chavat Da'at note 1. Siftei Da'at note 1. Chacham Adam, klal 53, note 6. Beit Yitzchak, vol. 1, in Amudei Zahav, note 1. Aruch HaShulchan ibid. Zivchei Tzedek note 3, who wrote that this is not in accordance with Beit HaLevi, who prohibits selling it to a non-Jew. And the fact that it is permitted to sell it to a non-Jew except [for the value of the forbidden portion], etc. — this applies when the situation is such that there is no concern that the non-Jew will sell it back to a Jew. Minhat Yaakov ibid. Zivchei Tzedek ibid.`,
    '5#_': `5) However, regarding a piece that is not worthy of honor, even a prohibited-in-benefit item is nullified, as per the law of dry-with-dry, which was explained at the beginning of siman 109. Siftei Da'at ibid. Beit Yitzchak ibid.`,
    '6#_': `6) Ibid. "Since if it were to be nullified it would be permitted, etc." — This applies specifically where, if it were nullified, it would be permitted for consumption. But where, even if nullified, it would not be permitted for consumption — such as a piece of basar b'chalav that became mixed with two pieces of neveilah — [the mixture] is permitted in benefit, because the Sages enacted the decree that a piece worthy of honor is not nullified only where, if it were nullified, it would become permitted for consumption. Peri Toar note 2. Chavat Da'at note 1. Siftei Da'at, end of note 1. Erech HaShulchan note 3. Aruch HaShulchan note 5. Zivchei Tzedek note 4, who wrote that this is not in accordance with Kehillat Yaakov who was stringent. And the reasoning is: since even if it were nullified the pieces would remain forbidden for consumption, if so it is only fit to be served with honor before a non-Jew — and anything that is not fit to be served with honor (meaning: after nullification) before every person is not called worthy of honor. Aruch HaShulchan ibid.`,
    '7#_': `7) Ibid., in the Rama's gloss. "And if it is doubtful whether it is worthy of honor or not, we rule leniently." Because the law of a piece worthy of honor — meaning the decree that it is not nullified by a majority — is d'rabbanan, and in a doubtful d'rabbanan case we rule leniently. And this leniency applies specifically to a doubt as to whether it is a piece worthy of honor; but if it is certainly a piece worthy of honor and there is a doubt as to whether it is forbidden, it is not nullified, as below in siman 110, seif 9 — see there. Shach, small note 2. Peri Chadash note 2. Shulchan Gavoah note 3. And so was written above in siman 100, notes 1 and 2, in the name of several poskim — see there.`,
    '8#_': `8) And this applies specifically when it is forbidden due to a doubtful Torah prohibition; but if it is forbidden due to a doubtful d'rabbanan prohibition, it is nullified. Taz, small note 1, and below siman 102, small note 3. Minhat Yaakov on the Torat Chatat, klal 40, note 20. Lechem HaPanim note 3.`,
    '9#_': `9) Ibid., in the Rama's gloss. "And if it is doubtful whether it is worthy of honor, etc." — This is not speaking of a case where the halachic decisor is uncertain whether it is a piece worthy of honor or not, for if so it would constitute a doubt arising from a lack of knowledge, and this is not called a doubt at all. Rather, it speaks of a case where we are uncertain about what the situation was at the time of the mixture — for example, where there are many pieces here, and among them are small pieces and a piece worthy of honor, and we do not know whether the forbidden item was the piece worthy of honor, and similar cases. Minhat Yaakov ibid., note 19. However, Peri Toar, note 4, wrote that even if one is uncertain regarding a piece whether it is worthy of honor, it is permitted — and this matter is not a doubt arising from a lack of knowledge, for in any case where in the eyes of those who are ruling the matter is not considered worthy of honor, we do not enact the decree, even though it is possible that in the eyes of others elsewhere it would be worthy of honor — for we only adjudicate the matter according to the time and according to the place. Thus, even if someone comes and says that it is a piece worthy of honor, if the people of that place are in doubt, it is regarded as a matter that is in doubt for all. End of his words. And so also wrote Mishbetzot Zahav, note 2, who wrote regarding the aforementioned words of Minhat Yaakov that the wording of the author does not indicate this. And so also wrote Zivchei Tzedek, end of note 7, that this is the primary ruling, in accordance with the words of Peri Toar, as is apparent from the general implication of the words of the poskim.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `Even if it is forbidden for benefit — and if a piece worthy of honor that is forbidden for benefit became mixed among several pieces that are permitted for benefit but nevertheless forbidden for eating — whether in that respect too it is not nullified and all are forbidden for benefit — see Pleiti.`,
    '1#ב': `For if it were to be nullified, and if it is a piece worthy of honor, and were it nullified there would no longer be a piece worthy of honor — nevertheless it is not nullified. See Pleiti.`,
    '1#ג': `And even if it is forbidden only by d'rabbanan authority — see Pleiti, where I deliberated and concluded that regarding a d'rabbanan prohibition that involves only a prohibition of eating, there is room to argue for leniency when there is an additional basis to permit.`,
    '1#ד': `This is a matter of doubt; see what I wrote below in siman 110.`,
    '2#א': `Unless it is forbidden, etc. And if one curdled cheese with milk from a treifah animal: although cheese is a piece worthy of honor according to local custom, nevertheless the milk of a treifah animal is not a piece worthy of honor, and it is permitted — unless there is enough treifah milk within it that it alone would suffice to curdle the cheese, in which case the entire cheese is forbidden and constitutes a piece worthy of honor. The later authorities agreed to this. See Pleiti. And regarding cheeses of Eretz Yisrael — even though the prohibition is due to absorbed taste, which is itself forbidden, and even if one were to say that the absorbed material itself is permitted — nevertheless the rabbinic decree was to forbid the cheeses and treat them as the substance of the prohibition itself. See Pleiti, where I brought proof for this; see there.`,
    '2#ב': `For the peel is not worthy of honor — even in basar b'chalav, where it was forbidden due to itself and not due to absorbed flavor.`,
    '2#ג': `Worthy of honor. And if the peel itself alone is a piece worthy of honor — the opinion of the Peri Chadash, and so too it appears the opinion of the Shach, is that it is not nullified. See Pleiti, that one may be lenient.`,
    '3#א': `For after it became mixed, since at the very moment it was mixed it was not yet a piece worthy of honor, it has already been nullified and is no longer significant enough to prohibit everything.`,
    '3#ב': `And similarly a whole lamb — even if they have already been cooked, since before cooking it would not in any case be considered a piece worthy of honor. And if so, one must say that even if one has removed from it the sinews, the forbidden fats, and the membranes, it is still called a whole lamb with respect to nullification.`,
    '3#ג': `A head, etc. But a head of a large animal implies that it is significant. The opinion of the Maharshal is that it is not significant. However, if the tongue is within it, the tongue is not nullified due to its significance, and the head does not diminish the tongue's significance.`,
    '4#_': `A piece of fat-covered hide, etc. The opinion of Lechem Chamudot is that it is nullified. And it appears that even though they cut pieces from it and use them to honor guests, nevertheless it is not considered a piece worthy of honor — for no individual piece alone is worthy of honor, only many of them together. Therefore each individual piece is called not worthy of honor.`,
    '6#_': `Or even when mixed with items not of the same species. The Issur V'Heter holds that the rule applies specifically when mixed with items of the same species. See Pleiti, where I deliberated on this matter and brought proofs in accordance with the words of the Issur V'Heter.`,
    '7#_': `We do not attribute the identity probabilistically. Even though when a piece falls into the sea we do attribute its identity and say that the forbidden piece is the one that fell — because it is no longer in the world and we need not rule upon it — here, however, since they are all present in the world and we must rule on this piece as well, whether it is treifah or kosher, we do not attribute its status probabilistically.`,
    '8#_': `We compare — see Pleiti: this applies specifically when one forbidden piece became mixed among two others, but if one became mixed among one, we do not rely on this comparison.`,
    '9#_': `A lamb's head — the Bach [holds that regarding] a lamb's head in which liquid was found, and the bodies then became mixed together, one should examine the spinal cord: if liquid is found there, we attribute the identification to the body that had liquid in its head, since the liquid extended to the spine as well. The Taz, however, questioned this, since it is not found in any early authority.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 101 in the Shulchan Aruch, seif 1) A piece worthy of honor has the law of beriah. And Terumat HaDeshen wrote that if after it was cooked it is not an important item — only before it was cooked it is also an important item and is not nullified. And one can bring proof for his words from the chapter of mixtures, where Tosafot challenge from the chapter "And is a large ox worse than other pieces," etc., and they explain that the Gemara's challenge is from dying sin-offerings, and among them are birds that were not worthy of honor — see there. And therefore one must say that they hold that since from the outset it was worthy of honor, it is also not nullified; for it deals with kodashim, and Tosafot already wrote that regarding kodashim there is no worthiness of honor, for the priests do not hold favor for one another; and if so, after nullification it is not worthy of honor. Or: since from the outset it was worthy of honor, it is also not nullified.`,
    '2#_': `(There in the hagahah) And even if it is forbidden only d'rabbanan it is not nullified. Rabbenu Yerucham proved this from the Rambam in Laws of Disqualified Offerings that chullin slaughtered in the Temple Court is forbidden only rabbinically; and in the second chapter of Avodah Zarah we learn that these forbid in any amount — chullin slaughtered in the Temple Court, etc. — from the reason that it is a piece worthy of honor for guests. If so, it is proven that even a d'rabbanan prohibition that is a piece worthy of honor is not nullified. And one might say that therefore the Rambam must hold that chullin slaughtered in the Temple Court is d'oraisa; for wherever it says "you shall not eat," benefit is also included, and proof is from nevelah where Scripture had to permit giving to a ger and selling to a non-Jew. The Gemara in Pesachim discusses whether we derive all prohibitions from "to the dog you shall throw it"; the Rambam rules that benefit is included from "you shall not eat," and in Laws of Idolatry rules like R' Yosi that only selling to a non-Jew is permitted. It appears the Rambam derives benefit from "that" (oto) and if so there is no source to forbid chullin in the azara — unless it is d'oraisa. After investigation, however, the Rambam may derive benefit from "you shall not give them a free gift" (lo techenam) regarding a non-Jew, not from "to the dog"; and the verse "sell to a non-Jew" establishes an extra positive prohibition. Thus the entire Pesachim discussion may be according to R' Yosi who learns from lo techenam, and there is no proof to all Torah prohibitions — we still need "to the dog." And since the Rambam did not cite that verse for chullin in the azara, he may hold it is d'oraisa. The Rosh in Eruvin explains the Mishnah of chullin in the azara as teaching that a piece worthy of honor is not nullified; if it were only rabbinic, the Mishnah of basar b'chalav would suffice. Thus the Rosh may hold like the Tosefta that it is not nullified, and that even benefit prohibitions are not nullified — unlike our conclusion that for eating prohibitions without benefit, even beriah and a piece worthy of honor are nullified according to Rif. Therefore one may be lenient regarding non-Jewish cheese when there is great loss, since it is a d'rabbanan eating prohibition. See the long discussion in the author's hagahah on siman 101 and siman 102.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 2) It does not have the status of a piece worthy of honor unless its prohibition is on account of itself, such as nevelah and basar b'chalav. One could say: how is it logical to say it is not nullified on account of a piece worthy of honor? For it can be established on this matter for the reason of each piece individually — that piece which has taste in it is forbidden and the rest are permitted. And one could answer that at first it absorbed taste from the milk, and afterward it fell into a pot that has sixty, and its taste was emitted — nevertheless that piece is forbidden, because we hold that if it is possible to squeeze it out it is forbidden; and the Mishnah that teaches "these forbid in any amount" regarding basar b'chalav comes according to the one who holds that if it is possible to squeeze it out it is forbidden. If so, Tosafot's challenge in the first chapter is from the chapter "And is a large ox worse," etc. — "and let it also teach nevelah, for it taught it there," etc. — and they answer: let it not also teach basar b'chalav, lest it teach us that if it is possible to squeeze it out it is forbidden. And it is impossible that it is not like the Mechaber in the first chapter, for if a piece fell into basar b'chalav there is a doubt whether it became forbidden, and it was mixed with nevelah or impure meat — it is impossible that it is not nullified also for benefit, or perhaps specifically for eating we say a piece worthy of honor is not nullified, but not for benefit. And it is impossible that for benefit too we say a piece worthy of honor is not nullified. One could say it deals with meat of a wild animal mixed with all of them where there is taste of milk, and therefore there is no way to establish the matter through tasting; and we properly say it is not nullified on account of a piece worthy of honor, even though nevertheless meat of a wild animal is also forbidden d'rabbanan — from where do we know for benefit? But the Mechaber in the first chapter decided the matter that we do not say a piece worthy of honor for benefit; therefore there is no reason to say it deals in such a case, and one must say as Terumat HaDeshen wrote, that even though it can be established on this matter, nevertheless we say it is nullified and tasting is not required; and if so, he properly said that in basar b'chalav it is a piece worthy of honor that is not nullified — meaning that one must taste whether there is taste in it or not. [Hagahah of the great Rav, etc., Mohar R. Shlomo HaKohen, may his light shine.]`,
    '4#_': `(There in seif 2 in the hagahah) And even a piece that was not salted is nullified, for its prohibition is not on account of itself, etc. Sha'ar Dura in chapter 40 wrote that a nevelah piece mixed with kosher pieces, none of which were salted, is not nullified if it was worthy of honor, even though it lacks preparation — after nullification it still needs salting; nevertheless lacking preparation is not like a chicken in its feathers. An incident came before Rabbeinu Tam who ruled thus; Hagahot ShaDa concludes, but I saw great authorities who say even unsalted nevelah is nullified. All this is because they hold it must be worthy of honor before Israelites specifically; if we say when nullified it would become worthy, they argue about unsalted pieces. The Mechaber in Darkei Moshe seif 1 implies one need not require worthiness before Israelites — even worthiness before a non-Jew suffices; unsalted nevelah not deveined that was mixed remains significant if it was worthy of honor. Peri Megadim and Noda BiYehudah discuss whether deveining is a great preparation like feathers; the Mechaber may disagree with Ran who requires worthiness before everyone. See Noda BiYehudah Yoreh Deah siman 16 and the author's long hagahah.`,
    '5#_': `(There in the hagahah) every piece that was forbidden only on account of a peel, etc. See Shach s.k. 6 that he means according to Mordechai, who apply a piece worthy of honor regarding a peel — see there; but one who examines Mordechai's words will see that he brought this law in the name of Riva and Rashba, and according to them they hold regarding hot meat that fell into cold milk that one needs sixty against the peel; unlike us, who hold like Rabbeinu Tam that one does not need sixty against the peel and it does not forbid the mixture at all — it is obvious that we do not apply a piece worthy of honor to it. Therefore the Rama wrote well.`,
    '6#_': `(Seif 3 in the hagahah) And so is the custom. See Shach s.k. 7 who cites Maharil and Bach that even a counted item (davar sheb'minyan) is not so — unlike the Torat Chatat who wrote that even if nullified on account of worthiness of honor, it is not nullified on account of being a counted item. Proof can be brought from the challenge at the beginning of the chapter of mixtures to nullify in the majority; Tosafot explain the challenge from dying sin-offerings, not from a stoned ox. Mordechai records an incident in France where Rabbeinu Tam ordered discarding one chicken and permitting the rest, saying that although a chicken is a counted item and important, it is not nullified — but he did not forbid on account of being a counted item; in his place chickens may not have been sold as a counted item. Tosafot in Yevamot equate a counted item with a piece worthy of honor. In our time chickens are not sold together in a basket, so feathers and sheep skin do not nullify as a counted item — see Semak. The Rama's custom except for chicken in feathers is correct.`,
    '7#_': `(There in the hagahah) except for a chicken in its feathers, which lacks great preparation. One could say: how does the Rama explain the Gemara's challenge in the chapter of mixtures, which challenges "and let it be nullified in the majority" — if one explains the challenge according to Tosafot, from dying sin-offerings, but from a stoned ox there is no place, for it is a piece worthy of honor — if so, a chicken in its feathers is also a piece worthy of honor. And if one explains according to the Rosh, that from a stoned ox too he challenges, for a large ox is worse than a piece worthy of honor — if so, why should a whole sheep be a piece worthy of honor? And one could say there is a difference between a whole sheep and a chicken in its feathers: Tosafot challenge according to what they explained, that the Gemara's challenge is from a bird sin-offering — for the Mishnah there teaches "and these forbid in any amount: metzora birds," etc. — if so, even a bird would be a piece worthy of honor. And they resolve that it deals with a sparrow, which is more a piece worthy of honor than doves and turtledoves. And the Rosh in the third chapter of Orlah wrote that metzora birds are different, for they are equated to impure birds, and therefore they are significant and are not nullified. And behold the Tur in the name of some who brought this ruling of the Rama in his name — one could say that Tosafot's explanation was considered strained, and therefore they distinguish between a bird in its feathers and a bird not in its feathers. If so, there it deals with a bird sin-offering that is still in its feathers — it is not a piece worthy of honor; but regarding metzora birds it deals after the feathers were removed — it is properly a piece worthy of honor. And incidentally, in the words of the Rosh in the third chapter of Orlah, one could say that his words settle what the world challenged in the passage in Kiddushin daf 57, where they say that from "this you shall eat," etc., we include the dispatched bird — and it is not nullified in the majority, etc. — see there at length. [Hagahah: the words of the author here are not understood to me — how is it proven from the verse that it is not nullified, for the Rosh only wrote that metzora birds, since the verse equated them to impure birds, are significant and are not nullified — and therefore he certainly means only d'rabbanan they are not nullified, but from the Torah even impure birds themselves are nullified one against two; for the world's challenge is according to those who hold that beriah is not nullified only d'rabbanan in general, etc. — end of hagahah of the great Rav Mohar R. Shlomo HaKohen, may his light shine, Vilna.]`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The law of a piece worthy of honor. And it has 9 seifim: A piece worthy of honor has the law of beriah — even in a thousand it is not nullified — and even if it is forbidden in benefit, since if it were nullified it would be permitted and worthy of honor: {Rama: And even if it is forbidden only d'rabbanan it is not nullified (Beit Yosef in the name of Ramban); and if it is a doubt whether it is worthy of honor or not we follow the lenient view (Arukh general rule 25) even if it is forbidden d'oraisa.}`,
    '2#main': `It does not have the law of a piece worthy of honor unless its prohibition is on account of itself, such as nevelah and basar b'chalav; but if it became forbidden because it absorbed taste from a prohibition and there was not sixty in it to nullify it — even for one who holds the piece itself becomes nevelah — it does not have the law of a piece worthy of honor: {Rama: And even a piece that was not salted is nullified, for its prohibition is not on account of itself but on account of blood absorbed in it (there and Taz siman 110); every piece that was forbidden only on account of a peel is nullified, for a peel is not worthy of honor (view of Tosafot and Hagahot Sh"D).}`,
    '3#main': `A chicken in its feathers that was mixed with others is nullified, for it is not worthy of honor before guests as it is; and even though after it was mixed they removed the feathers — and likewise it is not considered worthy of honor unless it is cooked; and likewise a whole sheep or an overly large piece is not considered worthy of honor, for there is no custom to place before guests a whole sheep or an overly large piece; and there are those who disagree in all of this: {Rama: And so is the custom except for a chicken in its feathers, which lacks a great preparation and the custom is that it is nullified (Tur in the name of R"IF and Mordechai and Sh"D); and likewise feet or a head whose hair was not singed — but if they were already singed they are considered a piece worthy of honor even if not yet salted, and even a whole sheep is not nullified (Hagahot Sh"D); however, feet of fowl except goose feet, and likewise feet of small livestock in these countries are not considered at all and are nullified; a head of fowl is not considered and is nullified (Arukh general rule 25).}`,
    '4#main': `Fat of the stomach lining is not worthy of honor (but goose fat skin is called worthy of honor and is not nullified) (there).`,
    '5#main': `Gizzard and likewise other intestinal organs are not worthy of honor.`,
    '6#main': `This that beriah and a piece worthy of honor are not nullified — only while they are whole; but if they were cut or crushed until their form was lost they are nullified and are no longer considered; and even if they were crushed after they were mixed with the permitted food (and even after the mixture became known) (Hagahot Sh"D and Darkei Moshe, unlike Or Zarua) — and this is when they were crushed without intent to nullify the prohibition; but if one intended for that it is forbidden to crush if it is his own, and likewise for one who crushed on his behalf (and there is no distinction whether they were mixed in their kind or not in their kind) (Darkei Moshe and implication of poskim, unlike Or Zarua).`,
    '7#main': `If one piece was cut — one may not rely to say that the forbidden one was cut and is nullified and all of them shall be permitted; rather only that one which was cut is permitted in any case: if it is of the forbidden, all of them are permitted; and if it is not of the forbidden, behold it is permitted; and even if a majority of them were cut one may not rely to say that the forbidden is from the majority that were cut — rather all the cut ones are permitted and the whole ones are forbidden.`,
    '8#main': `A gizzard that was found perforated and that chicken was mixed with others — one compares the fat in the gizzard to the fat of the chicken at the place of connection of the gizzard; and if they are entirely similar one permits the others (and likewise in all similar cases).`,
    '9#main': `The head of a sheep that was found tereifah and it is unknown from which sheep it is, and one wrapped the head around the neck of one of the sheep and the pieces were found similar and aligned well — one may rely on this to permit the others.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 101, Taz s.k. 4) And in my humble opinion, etc. Everything he wrote in this section is incorrect; for in truth the words of the Torat Chatat are puzzling, and the words of the Bach are correct, as I wrote in my books. And this is the language of the Torat Chatat, general rule 40, rule 2: he brought the language of the Tur that it does not have the status of a piece worthy of honor unless its prohibition is on account of itself, etc.; and he wrote regarding this, and so too Hagahot ShaDa and Tosafot and the Rosh in the chapter of Gid HaNasheh, and this is the consensus of the poskim, unlike Or Zarua at all — so it is regarding cheese curdled with treifah milk that it is considered a piece worthy of honor — end of his words. And this is his answer: for its prohibition is not on account of itself — end of the words of the Torat Chatat; and so too in Darkei Moshe. And it is puzzling, for in Or Zarua itself there he ruled like the Tur and the poskim, only that he wrote there rule 5, and this is his language: even a thing forbidden in benefit, such as basar b'chalav, is properly called a piece worthy of honor; and likewise even only half, or a quarter, or less than a quarter of cheese from a treifah animal — if there is so much that it is worthy of honor before guests, even in a thousand it is not nullified, as stated in Mordechai in the chapter Kol HaBasar from the responsum of Rabbeinu Shimon (and it is also in the responsum of Maimon siman 8); and even cheese curdled in a stomach forbidden from meat and milk is considered a piece worthy of honor, because meat and milk is also a prohibition on account of itself, as below; and even ordinary cheeses of non-Jews — although their prohibition is only through absorption — perhaps since the Sages forbade, even all ordinary cheeses are forbidden on account of concern for a prohibition that exists in some of them, and also their initial making was through a prohibition — it is as if born in prohibition. End of his words. Behold he only forbade cheese made from treifah animal milk, or cheese of a non-Jew; and he distinguishes that although we hold that the prohibition must be on account of itself, here it is forbidden; and it is strained to say that the Rama implied that nevertheless there is no reason to distinguish, for from his language it implies that he brought the consensus of the poskim and wrote "unlike Or Zarua," etc. — implying that the poskim are explaining against Or Zarua, and this is his answer. Furthermore, if so, he should have mentioned in the sections of the Torat Chatat that cheese of a non-Jew is not a piece worthy of honor; and furthermore he should have written "unlike Or Zarua, that cheese of a non-Jew is like a prohibition on account of itself for purposes of double doubt," etc. — see there. Therefore I did not delve to the bottom of the Rav's opinion in this, and everything Or Zarua wrote seems to me to be the main halachah — end of his words. And behold it has been explained to you that everything he wrote in this section did not help at all to settle the words of the Torat Chatat; and also what is implied from his words that the Bach rules regarding cheese curdled in treifah milk that it is a piece worthy of honor — this is not so, and he did not say that regarding cheese of a non-Jew it is a piece worthy of honor for the reason stated. And what he wrote that in the Torat Chatat general rule 43 it deals where there was no nullification in a majority of kosher items, and it does not deal there with worthy of honor — this is certainly not so, for behold he wrote there regarding this: but other d'rabbanan doubtful prohibitions that were mixed are permitted, for in d'rabbanan we do not require that the two doubts be on account of itself so much — so too in Or Zarua general rule 25, and he wrote there that nevertheless we require that it was mixed in a majority of heter, but not one against one — end of his words. Thus the prohibition of cheeses of non-Jews, even if mixed in a majority, does not help; and furthermore, there in that entire general rule it deals with an important item, and so too in Or Zarua itself it deals explicitly with a piece worthy of honor. And it appears straightforward that the Torat Chatat also concedes to the ruling regarding cheese of a non-Jew, and he did not challenge Or Zarua except according to what he understood that it deals with cheese curdled in treifah milk. And see what the Shach wrote in siman 110, rules 17 and 18.`,
    '2#_': `(There s.k. 10) And one may not be lenient in this. And it is possible to be lenient, for one does not honor guests with one fried piece, but with many pieces — if so, each piece by itself is not worthy of honor.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `(Siman 101) A piece worthy of honor, etc. The opinion of Terumat HaDeshen: if now, when nullified, it is not important, nevertheless before nullification it was important and is not nullified. The Taz in Orach Chayim siman 473 wondered: from where do we know this — perhaps we follow how it will be after nullification, whether to be lenient or stringent? Terumat HaDeshen has proof from Tosafot in Zevachim on "all sacrifices that became mixed," where the Gemara challenges "let it nullify in the majority" — it is a piece worthy of honor. Tosafot answer from dying sin-offerings with small birds; the challenge on a mixed sin-offering does not apply, for in kodashim there is no worthiness of honor. One cannot say kodashim never have worthiness of honor, for the Mishnah in Avodah Zarah teaches stoned ox, etc. Tosafot in Zevachim and Terumat HaDeshen are correct. Proof from Pesachim: five Paschal lambs, one invalid — we cannot stipulate, for breast and thigh belong to the priest; if the invalid were nullified one against two, there would be no prohibition of eating the Paschal offering improperly; therefore breast and thigh are a piece worthy of honor. See Tosafot at the beginning of "How do we roast." Peri Toar: if benefit-forbidden mixes with eating-forbidden, the Sages did not make worthiness of honor prevent nullification for benefit. In Avodah Zarah, basar b'chalav forbids in any amount — how is nullification possible if taste can be detected? It deals with beast basar b'chalav with fowl where taste cannot be distinguished; taste prohibitions for benefit still apply. Rambam holds beast and fowl are two kinds for worthiness of honor only in its kind — see siman 110. Therefore the Gemara's challenge stands; basar b'chalav teaches both benefit and eating. One should be stringent.`,
    '1#ב': `And even if the prohibition is only d'rabbanan, there is room to investigate whether a d'rabbanan eating prohibition is nullified. Yevamot teaches that a counted item in a d'rabbanan matter is nullified; terumah in our time is d'rabbanan and is nullified. Tosafot and Rosh discuss bundles of clover in kilayim of a vineyard. For us, who hold only "that which is counted" is not nullified, there is doubt which Tosafot reason applies. End of Avodah Zarah challenges using chametz on Pesach to teach nullification of a d'rabbanan important item. Beit Yosef infers from the Rambam that chullin in the azara has worthiness of honor; the Rambam may hold it is d'oraisa, but after analysis this is uncertain. Rambam in Terumot rules a sealed jug of terumah is not nullified even in a thousand — even d'rabbanan important items are not nullified. Rif holds that without benefit, even beriah is nullified. One may be lenient on non-Jewish cheese in loss cases.`,
    '2#א': `But if, etc. On this basis Torat Chatat challenged Or Zarua regarding cheese mixed with treifah milk; Peri Chadash and Minchat Yaakov explain Or Zarua as dealing with much treifah milk that was worthy of honor. I do not understand their reading of Or Zarua on cheese curdled in forbidden stomach — perhaps taste of meat absorbed in stomach reabsorbed in milk becomes a prohibition on account of itself; Or Zarua may hold curdling in basar b'chalav does not nullify. Nevertheless, non-Jewish cheese is a prohibition on account of itself because the Sages decreed it — we do not say taste follows the majority for absorbed blood in meat. Rashba's question on fish brine remains; the Sages forbade the brine path, not only the brine. Taz on curdling in neveilah skin is puzzling — sixty is required in milk as explained in siman 87.`,
    '2#ב': `But the peel, etc. Shach wondered why the Rama derived this from Tosafot in Chullin — only chein nafsho in half a piece is found, not worthiness of honor on a peel. Rabeinu Yerucham holds a peel can be a piece worthy of honor; if squeezing is possible it remains forbidden forever — unlike Tosafot who link piece becoming nevelah with squeezing. We hold like Rabbeinu Tam that a peel does not forbid wet mixtures without sixty; therefore worthiness of honor on a peel in dry mixtures has no place. Ramban ruled well.`,
    '3#_': `Except chicken in its feathers, etc. Shach challenged the Rama implying a counted item applies; Minchat Yaakov explained the Rama did not mean that. A sheep in its skin is obviously like chicken in feathers — see Peri Chadash and Semak. The Gemara's challenge in "all sacrifices became mixed" — R' Papa answers like the litra of pressed figs — does not resolve for sheep in skin or birds in feathers. Rabbeinu Tam permitted mixed chickens because they are not a counted item when sold in feathers. In our time fowl are not sold in baskets — feathers and skin do not count — see Semak.`,
    '6#_': `Not in its kind. Derishah uPerishah challenged Or Zarua that worthiness of honor applies only in its kind, citing beriah. The Mishnah in Avodah Zarah is the source; Acharonim in siman 110 wrote "in its kind" is not precise. Rambam in Orlah commentary: everything listed is in its kind with any amount; not in its kind requires sixty. He rules nevelah is nullified in slaughtered animal for tumah — important items are not nullified even for tumah; in not its kind, importance does not help. Cut fish may still be one kind called "piece of fish" unless very thin — see Or Zarua and Peri Chadash on siman 98.`,
    '9#_': `Head of sheep, etc. Peri Toar wants to be stringent: if we rely on resemblance for a basket of fish when impurity was not established, how can we rely here when impurity was established? One can answer: here all pieces are before us and one matches. Nevertheless signs are rabbinic; we rely on a simple sign plus rabbinic worthiness of honor. Where there is a majority kosher, Torah nullification applies; worthiness of honor is rabbinic. Correct.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `And if it is a doubt. See She'elat Yaavetz part 1 siman 218, where he elaborated whether the rule that an important item is not nullified is from the Torah or only d'rabbanan — see there.`,
    '2#_': `Such as nevelah. See in the responsa of Mahari Halevi siman 25, who wrote that even when it became nevelah through death it is worthy of honor. He further wrote that even if it is very lean it is worthy of honor — see there.`,
    '3#_': `And basar b'chalav. See in the responsa of Noda BiYehudah Yoreh Deah siman 64 regarding an incident where they baked kreplach with butter in nine copper pots, and within them were two in which kreplach had previously been baked with fat; and he wrote that although they greased the molds with fat and butter and it was visible, and therefore the whole became forbidden, nevertheless they are nullified in the majority, for those baked in the two are the minority against the remainder, and they are nullified as the law of dry-with-dry; and it is not called a piece worthy of honor, for although in basar b'chalav it is a prohibition on account of itself, nevertheless here the main substance is the dough, and basar b'chalav is absorbed — therefore it is nullified; one need only forbid according to the number of those baked in the two molds and permit the rest. And one could say it is considered not in its kind, for fat is not the same kind as butter, and sixty is required even in dry, and even for an absorbed prohibition, because if they cook them it will impart taste; nevertheless here it was only a d'rabbanan prohibition, for all the fat was goose fat, and in such a case the Shach wrote in siman 109 that even not in its kind is nullified in dry one against two — see there.`,
    '4#_': `In its feathers. See in the responsa of Noda BiYehudah Yoreh Deah siman 16, who wrote that a piece that was not deveined does not have the status of worthy of honor because there is trouble involved; and what is worthy for a non-Jew is not called a piece worthy of honor — nevertheless he did not decide this for absolute permission, but combined this reasoning as one branch with other reasons — see there. [And see in the responsa of Chatam Sofer siman 91, who wrote that a large piece, and also a live animal — we have Rashba, Ran, and Raah, and in my humble opinion the Rambam also holds like Raah, and so ruled the Mechaber, and the Rama only cites that there are those who disagree and wrote that so is the custom; and Peri Chadash fully agrees with the Mechaber — therefore in Frankfurt I added another leniency where there was some doubt whether the needle came out alive through both sides, for after rinsing in hot water a taste was almost sensed on the second side; I relied on the above and permitted with great loss of hundreds — see there.]`,
    '5#_': `And even though after it was mixed they removed it. See in the responsa of Radbaz (new responsa) siman 267, who wrote that if the tereifah was not found until after it became worthy of honor, it is not nullified — see further there.`,
    '6#_': `Other intestinal organs. Beit Yosef in the name of Taz regarding goose meat on the breast, etc.; and see Peri Chadash who disagrees and inclined to be lenient like Rashal; however in Bechor Shor in his novellae on Chullin daf 100 he inclined to be stringent like the Taz — see there.`,
    '7#_': `Fixing. See in the responsa of Shemesh Tzedakah Yoreh Deah siman 33, who challenged that the Mechaber's words contradict one another — for here and in the next seif it implies they are not similar prohibitions, whereas here it deals with whole sheep; also the chicken appears to deal when it is alive, for after cooking one cannot remove the fat; whereas above seif 3 the Mechaber wrote that it is not considered worthy of honor unless it is cooked, and likewise a whole sheep, etc. — see there; he offers many resolutions to this.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 101 seif 1 in the hagahah) Only d'rabbanan it is not nullified. That is, where it is forbidden by law — such as an egg of nevelah, or poultry meat in milk, and the like; unlike meat that disappeared from sight, which is only a mere stringency — if it was mixed in the majority it is permitted on account of a double doubt — so too the Shach in his general rules (rules 17, 18, 19).`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2) And kosher in milk. And if it became forbidden through salting — from the words of Menachem Tzioni (21 s.k. 10) it appears there is no status of a piece worthy of honor; and in my humble opinion his proof can be rejected, also from the words of the Taz s.k. 4, for it is clear that in basar b'chalav d'rabbanan there is also the status of a piece worthy of honor.`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 4) For there is a basar b'chalav prohibition here. I find this difficult: for above (seif 87) it is explained that curdling in the skin of a kosher stomach — if there is sixty in the milk it is permitted; thus through curdling it does not become basar b'chalav; and if curdled in the skin of neveilah it is forbidden only on account of neveilah, and it is no longer a prohibition on account of itself. Also according to what the Taz wrote (end of siman 87) that in impure meat with milk one cannot say each is permitted on its own — therefore it is not a prohibition on account of itself, and why should it also not be a piece worthy of honor? See there; if so, curdling in neveilah skin is more lenient, for it has no basar b'chalav status to judge it as a prohibition on account of itself, for one of them has a d'oraisa prohibition without the mixture — requires study. And on one difficulty one could say we are concerned perhaps there was sixty in the milk; but the second difficulty requires study.`,
    '4#_': `(Seif 7) If one piece was cut. If a d'rabbanan prohibition was mixed — piece in pieces — one could say according to the Mechaber above (siman 99 seif 6) that it is permitted to cut them l'chatchila so they will be nullified, since they are already mixed; only that it is not nullified on account of being worthy of honor — we are not stringent regarding mixtures of one against one; and cutting them to nullify is like adding and nullifying — investigate.`,
    '5#_': `(There) Permitted in any case. And why? One could say that if one of the pieces fell into a pot that has less than sixty portions of food and melted, it is permitted, since once melted its significance is lost and it is as if it was cut; and likewise if they removed it from the pot, nevertheless the pot is permitted, although the piece is forbidden — nevertheless one could say the gravy is permitted; see in Peri Megadim (siman 110 in the rules of double doubt, letter 10).`,
    '6#_': `(Seif 8) A chicken with others. Perhaps specifically with others, for from the Torah it is nullified in this case we rely on resemblance; but not one against one. However, from the proof of the Gemara from the case of a piece of fish above (siman 83 seif 4) it implies that even d'oraisa we rely thus.`,
    '7#_': `(Taz s.k. 15) And in my humble opinion there is nothing in this matter. And in the book Teshuvot Mateh Yosef (vol. 2 Yoreh Deah 6) he wrote that Rav Mohar Avraham, may his memory be blessed, in Salonika, and other rabbis, acted to permit.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["Forbidden in benefit, etc. It is explained in the Gemara in Avodah Zarah (daf 74a) that prohibitions of benefit — such as basar b'chalav and the like — if they are a piece worthy of honor, forbid their mixture also from benefit even in a thousand; and so is it among the poskim. And see Ran there. And see siman 109 s.k. 5. However, it is permitted to sell them to a non Jew except for the value of the prohibition within them, as I wrote below siman 110 s.k. 2:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["And if it is a doubt whether it is a piece worthy of honor, etc. For a piece worthy of honor that is not nullified in the majority is d'rabbanan, and a d'rabbanan doubt is decided leniently — and this is specifically a doubt whether it is a piece worthy of honor; but if it is certainly a piece worthy of honor and the prohibition is doubtful, it is not nullified, as below siman 110 seif 9 — see there:"]`,
    '2#א': `["And basar b'chalav. For since each one is permitted on its own, and through their joining together they became forbidden, the whole reverted to being as one body:"]`,
    '2#ב': `["It does not have the status of a piece worthy of honor. The reason is as the Hagahot ShaDa wrote in siman 41, and Rashba, Ran, and other poskim — because from the place of absorption the prohibition absorbed in it is not considered significant."]`,
    '2#ג': `["And even a piece that was not salted, etc. See above siman 99 seif 14 and what is written there:"]`,
    '2#ד': `["Every piece that was forbidden only on account of a peel, etc. It is written in Sefer Lev Chaim (daf 210b): and it requires study — from where was it forbidden on account of a peel? Only because it absorbed taste from another prohibition; and if so, even when the whole piece became forbidden, etc. end of his words. And to reconcile this: for we find it in basar b'chalav, etc. — investigate. In any case, what the Rav brought from Tosafot was not found; only that Tosafot at the beginning of the chapter of Gid HaNasheh (Chullin daf 96) wrote regarding a place of cutting, etc. — see there. And what the Shach wrote in the name of Lev Chaim is puzzling, for the peel itself has no prohibition upon it, etc. — investigate. And it appears that he holds that even if it is not rendered nevelah, nevertheless if it is possible to squeeze it out it is forbidden, as below siman 106, etc. — see there. And Tosafot in several places in Chullin and in the passage "a piece that absorbed," etc., hold that this depends on one another — it is impossible for the piece to become nevelah if it is possible to squeeze it out it is forbidden; we do not say thus; since the peel did not become nevelah, if it is possible to squeeze it out it is permitted and there is no prohibition upon it, and tomorrow it will return to being permitted — how can we say it is a piece worthy of honor? This is one point. Furthermore, since we hold like Rabbeinu Tam that a peel does not forbid its mixture in wet-with-wet, and one does not even need sixty, even though the taste is d'oraisa — how can we say that in dry-with-dry they will not be nullified on account of a peel, even in a thousand, on account of a piece worthy of honor, which is entirely a rabbinic decree? For regarding nosen taam d'oraisa we are not concerned, and for a rabbinic matter we are concerned — this matter has no relevance. And Rabeinu Yerucham and the other poskim who hold that a peel has the status of a piece worthy of honor hold thus because they hold like Riva that wet-with-wet forbids with nosen taam and requires sixty — and so too in dry. But we, who hold like Rabbeinu Tam that one does not need sixty against the peel and it does not forbid the mixture at all, it is obvious that we do not apply a piece worthy of honor to it. And the Rama ruled well:"]`,
    '3#א': `["A chicken in its feathers, etc. — it is nullified, etc. It is implied from the words of the Mechaber and the poskim that even a counted item (davar sheb'minyan) is not so — and as Maharshal wrote in his rulings and in his book Piskei HaGahot siman 53, that when it lacks a very great preparation it is also not a counted item; and this is clearly implied in Mordechai at the beginning of Beitzah — see there. And so is the opinion of the Bach, unlike the Torat Chatat who wrote in general rule 43 that even though it was nullified on account of being worthy of honor, it was not nullified on account of being a counted item — see above:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["And even though after it was mixed they removed the feathers. Nevertheless, since at the beginning of the mixture it was nullified, it does not revert to being prohibited when stirred. Sefer Mitzvot Katan:"]`,
    '3#ג': `["And likewise a whole sheep. Even if its skin was removed:"]`,
    '3#ד': `["Except for a chicken in its feathers. And a sheep in its skin, and the like:"]`,
    '3#ה': `["Except for goose feet. And the Torat Chatat wrote there in the name of Maharai that goose legs whose skin was not singed off them as people are accustomed to do are comparable to a chicken in its feathers:"]`,
    '4#א': `["Fat of the stomach lining, etc. And in Piskei Tosafot on Nedarim siman 80 I found that they wrote: and it requires study regarding the outer membrane of the stomach with its fat — perhaps it is worthy of honor — end of his words. And it appears that everything depends on time and place:"]`,
    '4#ב': `["But the skin of goose fat, etc. And Maharshal disagrees with this; and since these laws vary according to place and time, as the Rambam and poskim wrote, and as below at the beginning of siman 110, I have been brief in this, and everything depends on the judgment of the teacher whether it is considered worthy of honor in that place — if not, it is nullified; and if there is doubt, we are lenient, as above seif 1 in the hagahah:"]`,
    '5#_': `Gizzard, etc. It is implied from the language of the Mechaber that even the gizzard of a goose is included; and so is implied from the words of other poskim. But Maharshal wrote in his rulings and in his book Piskei HaGahot siman 50 that a gizzard is not nullified; and so is found in the name of Maharam in the hagahah below beginning "Maharam forbade a needle found in a gizzard," etc., that he ruled thus — end of his words (and after him the Bach followed). And incidentally he did not examine well, for one who examines there in siman 54 in that hagahah will see that Maharam's ruling applies specifically to a piece that absorbed a prohibition and there is not sixty in it to nullify it, etc. — see there at length.`,
    '6#א': `["Or they were crushed. And the Torat Chatat wrote in general rule 40 at the end: and it is implied in Or Zarua that the ruling that it is nullified if crushed means that no significant item remained there; but if a piece worthy of honor remained, even though it is nullified on account of beriah, nevertheless it is still considered significant and is not nullified on account of a piece worthy of honor — and this is sound reasoning, end of his words. And see further in Beit Yosef and Torat Chatat what they wrote in the name of Maharai:"]`,
    '6#ב': `["And even after, etc. See above siman 99 s.k. (9) [15] and siman 110 s.k. 39:"]`,
    '6#ג': `["And that they were crushed without intent, etc. See above siman 99 seif 5 and what is written there:"]`,
    '6#ד': `["And there is no distinction whether they were mixed in their kind, etc. Meaning that a piece worthy of honor, even when not in its kind, is not nullified even in a thousand — unlike Or Zarua who wrote that a piece worthy of honor is nullified only in its kind. And see above siman 102 s.k. 4. And see below siman 109 s.k. 8 — I wrote there how we find a piece worthy of honor mixed not in its kind. And see siman 110 s.k. 1:"]`,
    '8#_': `One compares the fat, etc. See above siman 83 s.k. 4:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `Only d'rabbanan. See siman 102 seif 8, where I wrote that even in a doubtful d'rabbanan prohibition there is stringency regarding something that has a permitted aspect specifically, and not regarding other things that are not nullified.`,
    '1#ב': `And if it is a doubt whether it is worthy of honor, etc. For this stringency that a piece worthy of honor is not nullified is d'rabbanan. And see what I wrote at the beginning of siman 100.`,
    '2#א': `And basar b'chalav. Because both of them became one body, and the proof is that one receives lashes from the Torah even if one ate only approximately half an olive-measure of milk and half an olive-measure of meat.`,
    '2#ב': `Because it absorbed taste, etc. For that which is absorbed is not worthy of honor. Based on this, Darkei Moshe and Torat Chatat general rule 40 wrote that cheese curdled in treifah milk that became mixed is not a piece worthy of honor, since the prohibition within it is absorbed, and not like Or Zarua at all — so is rule 5, where he was stringent also in this cheese on the grounds that it is as if born in prohibition. And Maharshal, may his memory be blessed, was puzzled at the Rav Rama in the Torat Chatat, for there in general rule 43 he ruled regarding cheese of a non-Jew that a double doubt does not help, since the prohibition is on account of itself, etc.; and based on this he ruled here too like Or Zarua that it is worthy of honor. And to me it appears that there it deals with cheese of a non-Jew in which there is concern that impure milk became mixed in, and there was no nullification in a majority of kosher items, and it teaches us that regarding double doubt they were stringent because, although all cheeses of non-Jews were forbidden from the outset on account of a d'oraisa doubt, as explained in Or Zarua general rule 26 at its beginning — this is why he calls it a prohibition on account of itself, meaning a certain prohibition, and it has no permission through a double doubt; for if from one doubt alone it is obvious that it is forbidden even though it is d'rabbanan, since there is concern that impure milk became mixed in and there would be a d'oraisa doubt, and even through a double doubt it is not permitted, for the first doubt is counted as certain, since the essence of the d'rabbanan prohibition here was in place of a d'oraisa doubt — and he did not mention worthy of honor at all. But here, where it was curdled in treifah milk and mixed in a majority — meaning that it could be permitted through nullification in a majority of kosher items, but you come to prohibit it on account of worthy of honor — in this I say: since it is absorbed, meaning that there is no mixture of treifah milk as a prohibition in it, but only that it was curdled in it — therefore it was only absorbed and is nullified, as will be explained shortly regarding the distinction between curdling in treifah milk versus treifah milk mixed in. And Maharshal wrote in chapter 25 siman 48, and this is the language of Mordechai in the name of Rabbeinu Shimshon son of Rabbeinu Avraham: who knows whether cheeses are as important as a piece worthy of honor, etc. And to me it appears that certainly there a piece worthy of honor does not apply to cheeses, for if it is moist it is not important, and even dry cheese that is important in our time — nevertheless it is not so important to eat enough for satiety, for it is hard on the body, and especially since many people do not eat it even under duress — until here his language. And his words do not seem right to me in my humble opinion, for we say in the chapter of Rabbi Eliezer that cheese even one day old is fit, and so wrote the long Torat HaBayit regarding cheese made from a treifah animal, which Beit Yosef brought in siman 21, that cheese is worthy of honor; and so explained the Rama in siman 81. Nevertheless, specifically there where the prohibition was on account of itself, for treifah animal milk was mixed in there and it was absorbed — it is worthy of honor; but in cheese absorbed from the prohibition we mentioned through curdling in treifah milk, the words of the Torat Chatat are correct that it was not a piece worthy of honor; and likewise in a mixture of impure milk — it is also not worthy of honor, for the Tur wrote in siman 115 regarding the reason for the prohibition of cheeses of non-Jews: perhaps they mixed impure milk in it, even though no impure milk is actually found — we are concerned that a little remains between its holes — end of his words. And it is obvious that regarding that milk between the holes it is not worthy of honor, since the body of the cheese is not made from it, unlike a mixture of treifah milk. And why did the Torat Chatat mention in general rule 43 "perhaps impure milk was mixed in," and a double doubt does not help, and he did not mention there regarding its being worthy of honor for the matter that nullification in the majority does not help — for there it would certainly be nullified if there is a majority, since it is only like absorbed. Nevertheless it appears to me that ordinary cheese of a non-Jew that was mixed among kosher cheeses we consider as an absorbed prohibition and it is nullified, and we do not say it comes from treifah animal milk, for we follow the majority, and most animals are kosher. And proof: many reasons are stated in the chapter Ein Ma'amidin why they forbade cheeses of non-Jews, as the Beit Yosef brought in siman 115, and he did not mention this reason that it is made from treifah, only "perhaps they mixed impure milk in it" — and this was not worthy of honor, and its law is like an absorbed prohibition as we said. However, one reason he counted there is that they curdle it in the skin of a neveilah stomach — and based on this it has the status of worthy of honor, for there is a meat-and-milk prohibition here. End of my words: in a place where it is known that they do not curdle in the skin of a non-Jew's animal, it is not a piece worthy of honor — so it appears to me.`,
    '3#א': `And there are those who disagree in all of this. Explanation: and it is reasonable to them that what they said "worthy of honor" does not mean that it must be worthy as it is now, but because of its measure they mentioned it — that it is large like this, worthy to give before a guest after it is cooked.`,
    '3#ב': `Except for a chicken in its feathers. Because it lacks a great preparation — it implies that if they already removed the feathers but have not yet taken out the innards and opened the belly, this is not considered lacking preparation and it is not nullified. And a sheep whose skin was not removed — it is obvious that it is nullified, a fortiori from chicken feathers; and so Maharshal.`,
    '3#ג': `But if they were already singed. Even though their hooves were not removed, one can trim them and it is not considered lacking great preparation.`,
    '3#ד': `Head of poultry. Maharshal wrote in the chapter of Gid HaNasheh siman 53 that the head of an animal is not considered, only the head of a calf — and certainly the tongue of an ox is considered, for Abraham honored the angels with three tongues.`,
    '4#א': `Fat of the stomach lining, etc. Maharshal wrote there in that siman that the outer membrane of a calf's stomach is considered, although other intestinal organs are not considered; and the Shulchan Aruch already wrote at the beginning of siman 110 that everything depends on place and time, and among us the outer membrane of a calf's stomach is important.`,
    '4#ב': `But the skin of goose fat, etc. In Or Zarua there in the gloss he brings that if the goose skin was cut into strips its significance is lost. Maharshal was puzzled there: since if cut it is not important, from what aspect would it be important when whole, etc.; and also in my humble opinion the opposite is astonishing, for the main honor is in the goose skin after they cut it into pieces for frying — our eyes see that in every place they honor important people with those fried pieces. End of my words: certainly it is a piece worthy of honor even when cut. And it appears therefore that the Rama did not bring in the Shulchan Aruch, and in Darkei Moshe, this distinction, although the Torat Chatat brought it — it is not the main point, and one may not be lenient in this — so it appears to me.`,
    '5#_': `Gizzard, etc. Maharshal wrote in the chapter of Gid HaNasheh siman 50, and this is his language: however, it appears to me that in most places I have seen that the gizzard of a goose is considered worthy of honor, and since the Rambam wrote that everything depends on time, certainly a goose gizzard is important and is not nullified — end of his words. It implies that of a chicken it is not worthy of honor; and so in Maharil in the name of Mohar R. Yaakov Tevel — this distinction. And see what I wrote in siman 75 that specifically for this purpose the gizzard is included among intestinal organs, as the Tur's words. Maharshal wrote there, and this is his language: the sages of Krakow ruled on goose meat on the breast called bilak — even though the goose was plucked, nevertheless the bilak is called worthy of honor. And to me it appears they ruled not according to law, although in truth in those places it is fit to honor guests with its roast; nevertheless, since the goose was plucked and its glory removed, its significance was lost, and it is somewhat like a beriah from which a little was removed — its significance is nullified. And here its significance is not nullified, since when it was not plucked it was very important — end of his words. And I wonder whether these words came from Maharshal's mouth, for they are very puzzling: that in a very large and important piece, if a little was cut from it, shall we say its significance was lost? Yet we say in the next seif that significance is not lost through crushing or cutting until its form is lost, etc. And this is not comparable to beriah, for whenever something is removed from it, it is no longer a beriah upon it. Therefore one may not rely on this for practice, only as the sages of Krakow ruled — that any piece currently fit to honor, even though it was formerly more important. It is written in the Levush that those goose livers that turned white from the fat are certainly important and are not nullified.`,
    '6#_': `And there is no distinction whether they were mixed in their kind. Explanation: regarding something that has a permitted aspect in siman 102 we say that not in its kind — even regarding something that has a permitted aspect — is nullified; and Or Zarua wrote in general rule 25 that a fortiori regarding other things that are not nullified, such as beriah and a piece worthy of honor, their doubt is permitted, as the Shulchan Aruch wrote at the beginning of siman 100, that if they were mixed not in their kind their importance does not help and they are nullified. And he disagrees with the Rama, for Rashba and the Shulchan Aruch in siman 100 regarding a pot of vegetables in which a beriah was lost — everything is forbidden — from which we learn that even not in its kind beriah is not nullified. And in truth his own words contradict one another, for in general rule 43 he wrote many laws regarding worms that forbid in mixtures, and the Torat Chatat wrote in general rule 40 that something that has a permitted aspect is different, for the importance is not on account of the prohibition itself but because there will later be permission for it — the Sages did not want it nullified; and everything mixed not in its kind — the permission is not called on the forbidden item but on the name of the item it was mixed into, and it is as if it has no permission — end of his words. And in siman 102 seif 1 I wrote in the name of Ran a more correct reason. Nevertheless I do not know how this hagahah applies here regarding crushing and nullifying the piece through cutting; and it appears there is a scribal error here, and its place is at the head of the siman in what the Shulchan Aruch wrote that it is not nullified — and here it has no place at all.`,
    '7#א': `If it is of the forbidden, all of them are permitted. And its explanation appears to be: if you wish to say that the forbidden item also enters into this doubt that perhaps it was cut. And Or Zarua wrote in general rule 25 that this law is only when there are sixty in the prohibition against the heter.`,
    '7#ב': `One may not rely to say, etc. Even though in every place we follow the majority, here it appears we should say the opposite: everything that changed from its former state is from the majority, and it is like everything that separated from the majority is presumed separated — and the forbidden item, which is the minority, did not change from its former state. So Maharai in Hagahot ShaDa. But one should not say the reason that every fixed item is like half against half, for here before you are two fixed portions — the cut portion and the portion that remained uncut — if so, it is more fitting to say the forbidden is in the cut portion, for this portion is the majority against the second fixed portion. Therefore one must say as Maharai resolved, that the reasoning is that it is in the remaining portion.`,
    '9#_': `Similar and well aligned. It implies that in appearance they must also be equal; and even though regarding the fat of the gizzard, the appearance of the fat alone suffices, here it is different, since it is possible to clarify also through their being well aligned in cutting — this too must be clarified, in my humble opinion. And Or Zarua wrote in general rule 52: now that we are not so expert, we have no permission to permit in such a case unless there is a great and recognizable difference — end of his words. And Maharshal, may his memory be blessed, wrote, and this is his language: and I received from one scholar who received from the mouths of scholars: if water is found in the head of one sheep that was tereifah in it and it is unknown from which sheep — one should examine the spine-cord of the sheep, and every sheep in which water is found in the brain in the head will have water in the cord extending from the brain to the spine, and we presume that the head is from that sheep to permit the others — end of his words. And in my humble opinion there is nothing worthwhile in this matter to permit a tereifah doubt, since it is not found in the Talmud and poskim.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 101 seif 1 in the hagahah) And if it is a doubt whether it is a piece worthy of honor, we follow the lenient view. The Taz's words here require study — he wrote regarding non-Jewish cheeses that they are not nullified because they are worthy of honor unless known they were not curdled in neveilah stomach, implying even a doubt of basar b'chalav forbids them. I did not understand his conclusion, for it is a doubt whether forbidden on account of itself — most poskim hold non-Jewish cheese is not forbidden because of stomach curdling. Taz himself in siman 81 is lenient on doubtful treifah milk cheese. Peri Megadim explains Taz as dealing when there is not sixty against the stomach — still puzzling. See Beit Yosef siman 115 and Peri Chadash.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2 in the hagahah) Every piece forbidden only on account of a peel is nullified, for a peel is not worthy of honor. Shach cites Lev Chaim: why forbidden on account of itself if it absorbed from another prohibition? And in Minchat Shai he left it requiring study. It can be forbidden on account of itself at the cut site — see Gemara regarding the fetus extending its hand, and detached limb siman 55. Cooking on Shabbat forbidden only on account of a peel is an example. Shach's case of hot meat into cold milk requiring sixty against the peel is difficult for those who hold dry nullification — see siman 105. Piece forbidden on account of peel nullifies in dry majority — Mordechai, Rabeinu Yerucham, Beit Yosef siman 105.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 3) And a whole sheep, etc. is not considered worthy of honor. See Chavat Daat who proved from Pesachim that a whole sheep is not nullified and is a piece worthy of honor — five Paschal hides mixed, a blemish found, all go to burning and require a second Pesach even before sprinkling, because at sprinkling time they were not fit. If a whole sheep is not nullified, this is difficult. Answer: they are forbidden because of invalid emurim mixed, not because of meat mixture — what is offered on the altar is not nullified; therefore they require second Pesach d'oraisa. Chavat Daat's rejection is answered. See Yad Malachi and Noda BiYehudah siman 16 on effort like chicken in feathers.`,
    '4#_': `(Seif 6 in the hagahah) And there is no distinction whether mixed in its kind or not in its kind. It may be not in its kind, e.g. fish kinds with the same appearance — siman 83 seif 4. Or Zarua and Derishah disagree whether cut fish are one kind; the hagahah follows that cut pieces can be two kinds — Gemara in Pesachim supports this. Peri Chadash on siman 98 agrees with Or Zarua for whole fish; cut fish may remain two kinds by name — see Tosefet Yom Tov on kilayim regarding camel wool with sheep wool.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `But if it became forbidden, etc. Noda BiYehudah siman 64 records a woman who lent two molds presumed dairy, baked kreplach with butter, returned them without saying so; later the borrower baked fat in nine molds including those two. He permitted nullification in the majority — kreplach are worthy of honor and a counted item, but the dough is the main substance and butter is absorbed — nullified; forbid according to the number of the two molds. He considered fat and butter not the same kind requiring sixty in dry. Shach siman 105 s.k. 23 is stringent on absorbed vessel taste; Magen Avraham is lenient. If molds were greased with forbidden fat before kreplach, there is concern of visible fat without sixty. Chatam David challenged Noda BiYehudah: taste can be detected in a spoonful. Answer: when eating one by one we say each is from the majority; Noda BiYehudah would forbid eating all together. Doubtful peel prohibition — fat taste may not be sensed. See long discussion on cooking milk in meat pot siman 103 and Noda BiYehudah on radish in fat. See also Givat Shaul and responsa on radish/meat pot.`,
  },
};
